00:04:50.077 --> 00:04:51.660
And one of the things
that we've built

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.410
is also this SD-WAN, which
creates a security connection

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:00.010
between your future
in the cloud and down

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.320
to your on-premise environment.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:05.530
We're also known for our
single sign on feature set,

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.360
especially around ADC.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.870
And I know Oncology Venture
is looking into using this.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:15.940
So we have really been a single
sign on company for many years.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:19.990
And we continue to build that
as Google introduced their IDP

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:26.272
solutions more and more
into to enterprise business.

00:05:26.272 --> 00:05:27.730
Another thing that
we are known for

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.770
is utilizing high graphic loads.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:34.960
So, for example, the signers
who needs a lot of GPUs

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.490
to do rendering in high end
graphics, most of the time

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.920
when you're working in a
simple framework structure,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.360
you don't really need
this heavy GPU loads.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.610
And it's increasingly
expensive, especially

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.070
around medical
research where they're

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:54.760
using this GPU to, for example,
look at high resolution

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:59.560
X-rays or biological scans.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.790
So these, the definition
on some of these scans

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.120
are very, very high.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.050
And one of the things that
we are using within Google

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:11.740
public Cloud is the
immediate GPU acceleration,

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.290
where we can actually
take a machine

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.900
and share it amongst many users.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:20.170
So sort of like you can
book a schedule or a time

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:24.100
where you need this
enormous solution

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.750
to do all your rendering.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:28.360
And thereby, you can save a
lot of money as a company.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:32.530
So very, very large investments
that we're doing together

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:33.700
will Google moving forward.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.360
So there's no doubt about it.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.540
This is, at the
core, what Citrix

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:41.100
do with this
partnership with Google.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.690
When we look into
the future, we've

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:47.130
always been known to have
very good app deliveration.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.710
We have been very,
very good at presenting

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.430
content to remote
users around the world.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.090
And remote when you're looking
at where everybody right now

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.460
is working-- we're
working remotely,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.390
and we're doing this
session remotely.

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:03.720
So it's at the core
of what Citrix does.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.300
But one of the things
we've always been known for

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.730
is also supporting
a lot of devices.

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:13.260
So in the old days, when you
needed to run an enterprise

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.220
solution and you had some
of the users using MacBooks

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.450
or Chromebooks, you
would need Citrix

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:22.290
to virtualize this enterprise
app to push it to any device

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:22.950
anywhere.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:26.310
So one of the things that we've
been doing for many, many years

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.260
is multiple device support.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:31.830
And just to give you
a glimpse into that,

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:35.050
Raspberry Pi is becoming a
mature enterprise product.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:38.370
And we're actually supporting
with our Workspace client

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:42.120
to Raspberry Pi, which in
some scenarios-- and you

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.140
might laugh about it.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.380
But I went to the
hospital the other day.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.870
And in the heart cardio
ward, they actually

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:51.480
had a small Raspberry
Pi 4 monitor just

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:55.980
showing the remote EKG
of the patients walking

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.060
around the ward.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:00.240
And that was being presented
on a Raspberry Pi hooked up

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.620
to a monitor.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:05.040
So it's really becoming
any device anywhere

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.300
and any application available.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:08.880
And as we look at
application, we

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.340
are moving more and more
into SaaS application.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.650
Not necessarily having
virtual applications,

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.790
we're also looking at how we can
do all the things that we are

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:21.570
known for-- security,
analytics, and single sign on--

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.370
with local apps,
which are pushed out

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:29.090
alongside perhaps virtualized
app or even SaaS apps.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.640
And around that, we have built
this secure digital perimeter

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.270
based on these 25 years within
the enterprise space, where

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:37.980
we've supported a
lot of the largest

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.059
companies around the world.

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:44.490
In my own country, I can't
go to any hospital in Denmark

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:50.550
without coming into a doctor who
knows about Citrix and knows--

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.290
when I tell him I've
worked for Citrix,

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.450
he knows exactly what
it is because it's

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.510
the first thing he sees when
he logs into a computer.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.340
So that trust is
really something

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.750
that we take at heart.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.470
And when you hear about Ulrik's
story within health care,

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.600
why he chose Google
Cloud, there's

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.020
a very interesting perspective
about the two technologies

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:12.150
being HIPAA compliant.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.340
But I'll leave that for Ulrik.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.130
So what we are doing with
the digital workspace

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.590
is enhance the experience,
the productivity.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.230
So that means that
we are constantly

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.930
monitoring the
change in location

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:29.420
the user has, so we can do
something about the performance

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:33.840
and maintain a high performance
from the users having

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.160
to do the job.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:38.160
We are also looking at the
way that we collaborate.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.047
So a lot of users today
have too many apps.

00:09:41.047 --> 00:09:42.630
And when they need
to make a decision,

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.750
they need to log in to
different application.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:47.490
And then they open
their mail and then

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.420
write a response to that.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:53.550
So that world we are
trying to compress

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:57.780
into what we call
microapps, or microservices,

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:00.390
and being able to
take a lot of content

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:05.780
switching between applications
into this microapps world.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:07.950
And that is something
that we've taken the lead

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.110
on in actually designing these.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.960
And we have full interaction
with other vendors

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:14.860
out there, including Google.

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:18.660
So when you are looking at using
enterprise application running

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:21.510
in Google Cloud, we're
able to take that data

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:23.820
and say, OK, 80% of
the time, you just

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.280
need this information
and that information,

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:29.010
collaborate together, and
then have this approve,

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:33.730
or disapprove, or deny,
transfer of funds, or so on.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:36.750
So simple actions is
something that we want

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:40.540
to do within our Workspace.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:44.400
So how does it look what
we're actually delivering

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.650
with Google Platform?

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:48.750
You can divide Citrix
into two world.

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.580
One is the control
plane, and one

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:54.510
is the workhorse behind the
curtain, so to speak, our VDAs.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:57.000
Our VDAs are the
ones who are actually

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.780
doing the heavy lifting
and doing the presentation.

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:04.260
The control plane is just doing
the introduction of the users,

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:06.510
and what rights the user
have, and all the single sign

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.400
on within the workload service.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.650
And all that I will
show a little bit more

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:14.850
about how that's
running in Google Cloud.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:18.010
We have Citrix Access Control.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.060
So, for example, if
you're in one location,

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:25.270
you can save things
locally on your device.

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:28.200
But when you're outside
on another location

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.300
or outside your zone--

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:33.335
for example, if you're in Europe
and you work out of the US,

00:11:33.335 --> 00:11:34.710
when you're in
Europe, you're not

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:37.330
allowed to save on
your local device.

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:38.820
So the whole access
control thing

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.760
we have actually integrated
into G Suite, which

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:47.325
is where you just take the
security part of G Suite

00:11:47.325 --> 00:11:50.600
and lift it to another
level using access control.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:53.400
And this is what a
lot of our customers

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:56.040
really appreciate about
Citrix, that they can say,

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.140
well, you have one
user scenario when

00:11:58.140 --> 00:11:59.540
you're working
inside the company

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.613
and you have access
to these shares.

00:12:01.613 --> 00:12:03.030
And then you have
another scenario

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.100
when you're working at
a different location,

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:08.920
or even borrowing somebody's
device to do work.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:10.770
And, of course, we
support the story

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:14.910
with our Citrix Workspace app
for all of the Google devices

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.420
that you can think of.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:22.780
So even down to Chromebase
devices, Android devices,

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:24.540
and so on.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:28.860
We integrate our enterprise
device management

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:31.515
into the Citrix
G suite solution.

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:40.580
So the Citrix resource
location on Google Cloud,

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:41.730
I won't go so much--

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.390
I would rather have
Ulrik talk a little bit.

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:47.630
But what it actually
does is that you take--

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:50.690
the users are creating
a session which

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:53.330
hits the workspace at
the gateway services.

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:55.170
The control plane
simply says, well,

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:56.600
you have the right to this.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:59.620
And then it introduces you
into the workloads service

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:00.980
near these Cloud connect.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:03.920
Now the Cloud connectors
are crucial technology

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:06.080
when talking about a
hybrid solution where

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:10.920
you both coexist on premise
and you work in the Cloud.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:12.890
So that's where the
Cloud connectors come in.

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:17.540
And it actually defines
which applications

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:19.280
should be presented
to you, which

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:22.580
files shares, which
Cloud-based file shares

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:24.380
you should have available.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:28.070
And it presents the
best VDA for the desktop

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:30.050
that you're actually running.

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:32.580
But I'll let Ulrik cover
that a little bit more.

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:36.320
Now, this solution
from Oncology Venture,

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:39.500
I actually talked to
Ulrik over a year ago.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:44.330
And I was so fascinated
by the solution,

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:46.770
because it can actually
change people's lives.

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:50.410
We've all had people who
are affected by a cancer.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:54.050
And what he told me was
sounded like science fiction.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:57.180
But I believe we've pulled
it down to earth, Ulrik.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:59.120
So maybe you can
tell a little bit

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:04.310
about Oncology Venture and the
solutions that you've built.

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:07.680
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN: Yes,
thank you very much for that.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.960
So let me start by introducing
the company Oncology Venture.

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:15.110
So Oncology Venture
is a med tech company

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:19.190
based in Copenhagen with
employees in the US--

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:22.730
all the way up and down
the east coast of the US.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:26.090
We have developed a platform
that can predict the best

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:29.840
treatment for cancer patients.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:34.120
So our platform is
called DRP and is

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:41.200
based on the full transcriptome
of the messenger RNA.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:44.950
That combined with tumor
data from other patients,

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:46.792
we have created
an algorithm that

00:14:46.792 --> 00:14:48.250
can predict the
treatment that will

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:51.560
benefit the patient the most.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:56.850
So our employees work on
many different devices,

00:14:56.850 --> 00:15:01.850
anything from Windows
machines to Mac, and also

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:04.720
on mobile devices.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.200
So we spent a great
deal on support

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:13.130
before we introduced
Citrix to our employees.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:16.180
They're also working in
different time zones,

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:20.680
so the support would
be quite difficult.

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:25.020
So we decided to offer
this standard solution,

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.840
the standard desktop, with
all the right tools and all

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:31.350
the right applications that they
needed, so it was easy for them

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:33.360
to get things done.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.370
By making this
centralized solution,

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:40.015
we also managed to
secure our files.

00:15:40.015 --> 00:15:44.460
Ejal just mentioned that with
the digital secure perimeter,

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:51.310
because no files left our own
data center or the Google Cloud

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:53.370
data center.

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:59.950
So that make the
solution very secure.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:05.370
And we also heard about before,
we had some key challenges,

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.260
if you can say that.

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:14.220
So we experienced
some poor performance

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:17.970
with some other
vendors, cloud vendors.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:22.490
And we actually start to look
into this on-premise solution.

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:24.900
But we ruled that
out quite fast,

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:28.990
because the initial
cost to build own data

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:31.110
center with all that--

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:34.740
what that took with
cooling and all that,

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.760
that was just way too expensive.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:41.110
So we started to look
into another solution.

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:44.310
And we ended up with
Google Cloud and Citrix,

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.950
because they gave us the
features and the performance

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:49.800
that we needed.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.890
We also had limited scalability
before we introduced

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:57.270
this Citrix and Google
Cloud to Oncology Venture,

00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:00.900
because we didn't have
anywhere to build, actually.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:04.020
We also had many
different cloud solutions.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:08.339
We had file solutions,
and they were kind of

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:09.720
scattered all over the place.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:15.050
So we didn't have any standard
or any structure in it.

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:18.800
And with this kind of
data that we handle,

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:24.150
we need-- we have very
strict security requirements.

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:28.530
So we needed to
step up our A game

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:31.990
to meet those requirements.

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:36.450
So with Citrix and Google
Cloud, we immediately

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:40.860
saw that we could get those
features at that performance

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.310
that we needed.

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:49.020
So we were also able to
implement all the security

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:51.870
things that we also needed.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:56.430
And with the partnership
between Citrix and Google,

00:17:56.430 --> 00:18:02.650
we were able to scale this
solution on Google Cloud.

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:07.390
So I want to talk a little
bit about the Workspace app.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:10.060
Ejal mentioned
that just earlier.

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:16.650
So we rolled out Citrix
Workspace app to make it easier

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:20.440
for our employees to get
access to their data.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:22.710
So we have people
sitting around the world,

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:24.970
working on our solutions.

00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:27.180
And also, we have
people traveling.

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:30.240
So we wanted to give them
the best and most easy

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.310
access to all of
our applications

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:34.830
and all to our files.

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:40.050
Also, another thing was
that when we had new hires,

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:41.760
they were productive
from day one.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.800
All they needed was
the Workspace app

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:46.410
and then just logged in,
and they had all their apps

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:51.160
and all their files right
there, right in front of them.

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:55.060
So that was a lot easier for us.

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:57.980
So if you look at the
solution as a whole--

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:00.490
Ejal mentioned this also.

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:06.780
So all of the users are
sitting on their own device,

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:09.000
and all they have is
the Workspace app.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:11.730
Now, they connect
from the Workspace app

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:16.290
through the Gateway,
which is in Citrix Cloud.

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:18.900
From the Citrix
Cloud, the Gateway

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:21.120
connects to the Google--

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:23.850
sorry, the Citrix
Cloud Connectors.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:28.710
And that-- and the
Citrix Cloud Connectors

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:32.250
communicates with all the things
that you have in your back end.

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:34.800
That could be your Active
Directory and all the server

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:39.450
VDAs, so all the workloads
where your applications are

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:42.250
and all your files are.

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:49.380
So with this solution, we
were able to give people

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:53.160
secure access and also
be able to scale up

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.290
the environment as needed.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:58.980
Because sometimes, we
had people coming in--

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:01.290
consultants working on
different projects--

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:06.720
and we could easily spin up some
extra workloads in Google Cloud

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.850
with Machine
Creation Service that

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:11.550
was introduced by Citrix
about six months ago,

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.770
if I remember correctly.

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:21.630
And we could get access to
these external consultants

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:22.970
right away, from day one.

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:30.660
So why did we choose
the Workspace?

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:33.300
Well, for us, the
most important thing

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:36.930
was, it gave us easy onboarding.

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:40.410
So it was very easy for
us to onboard new hires.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:44.550
And also, when things changed--
if we got a new application--

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:46.680
it was easy for us
to push that out

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:49.450
to our employees
around the world.

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:55.950
It was also easy for us to get
external consultants access

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:59.770
to our own system.

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:03.780
Another thing was that
we got a lot less support

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:08.220
from our employees, and we
had everything in one place.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:10.830
So all our
applications and files

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:13.410
could get-- people
could get access

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.350
to those files and
applications from one place.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:21.640
At the same time, it kept
all of our data safe.

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:25.660
So with Citrix, we can
provide a much better service

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:28.930
to our users and to
our partners, which

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:32.290
I think is a key element
if you want to stand out

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:35.810
as a professional company.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:38.600
So why Citrix in Google Cloud?

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:43.760
So we started out to look at
apps and on-prem solution,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:49.010
and because of the cost, we
just didn't go with that.

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:54.110
So we had to buy a lot of
stuff to get that rolling.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:56.870
So we looked at
different public clouds,

00:21:56.870 --> 00:22:01.340
and we ended up with Google
Cloud as the best fit for us.

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:04.820
The reason were that
Google Cloud was very

00:22:04.820 --> 00:22:07.170
simple to get started with.

00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:11.390
And for us, it had
the best performance.

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:17.540
Now, one other thing was also
the Citrix-Google partnership.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:20.340
And Citrix was really helpful.

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:23.000
They were very serious about
the partnership with Google

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.160
as well.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:26.030
So, as I mentioned
before, Citrix

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.940
released Machine
Creation Service,

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:33.290
which made it a lot faster
for us to manage and update

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:36.620
our image and being able to
push out new applications

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:39.770
to our employees.

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:45.290
One other thing that the
partnership came up with

00:22:45.290 --> 00:22:48.600
was something called Autoscale.

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:55.590
So, with Autoscale, we
only spin up the resources

00:22:55.590 --> 00:22:58.090
that we need in Google Cloud.

00:22:58.090 --> 00:23:02.260
So when we don't any
need for any resources,

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:06.030
Autoscale is going to
turn off the machines

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:07.680
that we're not using.

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:11.320
So over-- I tried it
out for about a month.

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:16.380
And in the course of that time,
I believe we saved about 40%

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:19.590
on our cost on resources
in Google Cloud.

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:24.180
So if you're thinking about
moving to a public cloud,

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:26.610
it's a very good
feature to have,

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:28.980
because it's going to
save you a lot of money.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:30.060
EJAL BRACHA: So Ulrik--

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:33.680
so, for example, a lot of
people who go to public clouds,

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:35.640
they're afraid of
the cloud economics.

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:39.810
Because once you spin up
a machine, the economics,

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:40.730
it's just--

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:43.122
the meter is just ticking.

00:23:43.122 --> 00:23:44.080
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN: Yes.

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:47.485
EJAL BRACHA: So Autoscale
help you control your--

00:23:47.485 --> 00:23:49.110
you could sleep better
at night knowing

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:52.650
that, if nobody was
using the system,

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:55.140
the workload servers
would be shut down.

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:58.170
And Machine Creation Service
gave you the agility.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:23:59.752
Is that right?

00:23:59.752 --> 00:24:01.210
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN:
That is correct.

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:06.660
So when-- well, since we have
users in different time zones,

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:10.660
we almost always have
users on the system.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:15.190
But sometimes, we had
more than other times.

00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:20.740
And we-- the Autoscale
feature scaled up

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.630
the number of workloads
that we had for the user.

00:24:23.630 --> 00:24:26.260
So as soon as the
number of users--

00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:30.430
when they logged off the
system, Autoscale automatically

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:33.350
scaled down the
number of workloads.

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:36.620
So it simply just
removed the workloads,

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:39.970
which means that you don't
have to pay for those resources

00:24:39.970 --> 00:24:41.710
anymore.

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:45.190
So that saved us quite a
lot of money, actually.

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:46.090
So--

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:48.350
EJAL BRACHA: And with
Machine Creation Service,

00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:50.680
I remember you told
me that it actually

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:55.090
retains the metadata of the VMs
that you have in Google Cloud.

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:56.957
So when it needs to
spin up, you know

00:24:56.957 --> 00:25:00.060
that it's the same type
of VMs that actually gets

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:01.932
spun up, which is really cool.

00:25:01.932 --> 00:25:02.890
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN: Yes.

00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:05.230
Not only that, it
keeps the metadata.

00:25:05.230 --> 00:25:07.990
So it also--

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:08.980
EJAL BRACHA: Firewall.

00:25:08.980 --> 00:25:10.772
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN:
Firewall rules, exactly.

00:25:10.772 --> 00:25:12.820
So when you spin up--

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:16.000
let's say that if you spin
up like four new workloads,

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:21.250
those workloads will get
the same metadata pushed on

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.980
to those VMs, which
means that you don't have

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:29.230
to go in afterwards
and do all the firewall

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:34.360
rules for those four VMs,
because the system already

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:35.350
knows that.

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:40.480
So it runs pretty
much by its own.

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:44.090
So you don't have to
go in and do anything.

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:47.380
It can actually pretty much
do all of it by itself.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:51.220
So that's actually one of
the very unique features

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:56.170
about running this
system in Google Cloud.

00:25:56.170 --> 00:25:58.720
So good question.

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:00.960
EJAL BRACHA: So tell me
about the cancer scanning,

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:04.872
the really exciting thing that
you're doing in Google Cloud.

00:26:04.872 --> 00:26:07.080
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN: So, as
I mentioned before, we also

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:12.660
run our DRP platform
in Google Cloud.

00:26:12.660 --> 00:26:16.680
Well, we used to run
the DRP platform, which

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:22.560
is our Drug Response Predictor.

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:26.470
And we were able to
run, I would say,

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:33.830
about 8 to 12 samples
at the same time.

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:38.240
And we knew that we wanted to
scale it up and be able to run

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:40.470
more at the same time.

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:46.040
So we decided to look into,
could we run these samples

00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:50.300
in a containerized environment.

00:26:50.300 --> 00:26:52.940
Because if we could do that,
it would be a lot easier

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:55.590
to scale it up and down.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:27:02.630
So, actually, we had a project
with about 1,500 samples.

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:06.980
And it took us about two
and a half to three weeks

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.320
to run those samples.

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:14.290
And I believe that in the new--

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:16.310
on Google Cloud, if
we run them there,

00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:20.870
we can do it in probably two
or three hours, I'd guess.

00:27:20.870 --> 00:27:23.420
So going from two
and half weeks,

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:26.270
three weeks, to
two or three hours

00:27:26.270 --> 00:27:30.050
for the same amount of work--

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:32.580
so that's a big deal.

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:36.680
So we actually went ahead
and built this solution

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:37.640
on Google Cloud.

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:40.700
And Citrix has been a
big part of that as well,

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:43.200
to secure this application.

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:45.800
As I mentioned before,
this kind of data,

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:47.240
we need to lock that in.

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:50.120
We have to make it safe.

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:53.960
So Citrix has a big part of it--

00:27:53.960 --> 00:27:55.800
has a big part in it.

00:27:55.800 --> 00:28:01.990
So we introduced Citrix
ADC to the solution.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:04.770
And we're going to talk
a little bit about that.

00:28:04.770 --> 00:28:06.610
So--

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:10.800
EJAL BRACHA: But before you
do that-- so the hospital gets

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:13.530
a cancer sample
from a patient, they

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:16.060
submit it into the
cancer scanner,

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:21.250
and the cancer scanner opens a
container for that one sample.

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:25.800
It has a scanning mechanism
of what kind of drugs

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:29.530
has the best opportunity to
cure this kind of cancer.

00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:31.560
And then a report comes
out the other end,

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:33.900
saying, your doctor--

00:28:33.900 --> 00:28:35.460
I would recommend
this treatment,

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:38.672
or this treatment has this
kind of probability of success.

00:28:38.672 --> 00:28:39.630
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN: Yes.

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:42.520
EJAL BRACHA: This has the
least success criteria.

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:45.600
So it's both best success
and least success,

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:48.180
is the report that comes
out at the other end.

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:49.930
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN:
You can say that.

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:53.790
So what we do is, once
we get the end results,

00:28:53.790 --> 00:29:01.230
we put in a sample into the--

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:03.780
first, it has to go
through our own wet lab.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:07.950
And then we-- once
that is done, we

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:12.090
can put it into the system
that we have, our DRP system.

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:14.640
And the end result
from the DRP system

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:17.910
is a report saying
that which drug will

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:21.060
benefit the patient the most.

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:23.930
That's what we get.

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:24.430
And--

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:25.186
EJAL BRACHA: Super.

00:29:25.186 --> 00:29:25.920
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN: And--

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:28.200
EJAL BRACHA: And before,
you could do very, very few.

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:32.370
And now you just scale
it up in the cloud.

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:34.530
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN:
We can scale this up.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:40.740
We can now run hundreds,
maybe thousands of samples

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:41.780
at the same time.

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:44.990
So yeah, it's quite big a deal.

00:29:44.990 --> 00:29:46.055
So yeah.

00:29:46.055 --> 00:29:47.680
EJAL BRACHA: And the
part of the Google

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:50.170
Cloud that you've loaded
your solution into

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:52.480
is HIPAA approved.

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:53.410
Is that correct?

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:55.570
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN:
Yes, that is correct.

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:59.090
EJAL BRACHA: So that's huge
that, just based on that,

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:02.080
you can go through
all the FDA stuff

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:05.850
and say, well, we're running it
in a HIPAA-approved location.

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:07.270
ULRIK CHRISTENSEN:
Yeah, the FDA--

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:09.170
